(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["da-easy-2026-003"]={"v":1,"id":"da-easy-2026-003","title":"Advance Level Test-3 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 DA","sourceTitle":"Advance Level Test-3 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 DA","branch":"DATA SCIENCE AND ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE","code":"DA","provider":"DVRUO EASY","providerKey":"easy","series":"GATE 2026 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI","seriesId":"da-easy-2026","num":3,"category":"advance","year":"2026","format":"A","durationSec":10800,"info":{"meta":"Package: GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI • Questions: 65","package":"GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI"},"source":"DATA SCIENCE AND ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE/MADE EASY/GATE_2026_+_GATE_2025_Online_Test_Series_Data_Science_and_AI/006_Advance Level Test-3 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 DA.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Count the number of squares in the following figure:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5d673861005a2eca5f5b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"18"},{"key":"B","html":"14"},{"key":"C","html":"10"},{"key":"D","html":"9"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/02/02f0ebec61bb871832b2.png\" /></div>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Find the odd one out from the given sequence:<br />\n1, 5, 14, 30, 50, 55, 91</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"5"},{"key":"B","html":"50"},{"key":"C","html":"55"},{"key":"D","html":"91"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d8/d877bc9077fbb88d7e98.png\" /></div>"},{"n":3,"type":"NAT","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A man standing on the bank of a river observes that the angle subtended by a tree on the opposite bank is 60°. When he retries 36 m away from the bank, he find the angle to be 30°, the breadth of the river is ____ m.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[18,18],"answerText":"18","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>18 (18 - 18)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ea/ea9a13046acff988548e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>In a race of 200 m, <em>A</em> can beat <em>B</em> by 31 m and <em>C</em> by 18 m. In a race of 350 m, <em>C</em> will beat B by</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"22.75 m"},{"key":"B","html":"25 m"},{"key":"C","html":"19.5 m"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/62/62534949b5b66d9b40aa.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b6/b6a9430938bda3b9cb50.png\" /></div>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Astronomer: Most stars are born in groups of thousands, each star in a group forming from the same parent cloud of gas. Each cloud has a unique homogeneous chemical composition. Therefore whenever two stars have the same chemical composition as each other, they must have originated from the same cloud of gas.<br />\nWhich of the following, if true, would most strengthen the astronomer’s argument?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"In some groups of stars, not every star originated from the same parent cloud of gas."},{"key":"B","html":"Clouds of gas of similar or identical chemical composition may be remote from each other."},{"key":"C","html":"Whenever a star forms, it inherits the chemical composition of its parent cloud of gas."},{"key":"D","html":"Many stars in vastly different parts of the universe are quite similar in their chemical compositions."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nIt is given that each cloud has a unique homogeneous chemical composition and the stars originated from the same cloud of gas have the same chemical composition. Thus, it can be concluded that whenever a star forms, it inherits the chemical composition of its parent cloud of gas. Hence, option (c) is correct.</p>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>People tend to estimate the likelihood of an event’s occurrence according to its salience; that is, according to how strongly and how often it comes to their attention. By placement and headlines, newspapers emphasize stories about local crime over stories about crime elsewhere and about many other major events.<br />\nIt can be concluded on the basis of the statements above that, if they are true, which of the following is most probably also true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The language used in newspaper headlines about local crime is inflammatory and fails to respect the rights of suspects."},{"key":"B","html":"The press is the news medium that focuses people attention most strongly on local crimes."},{"key":"C","html":"Readers of local news in newspapers tend to overestimate the amount of crime in their own localities relative to the amount of crime in other places."},{"key":"D","html":"None of the events concerning other people that are reported in newspapers is so salient in people’s minds as their own personal experiences."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nWe know that newspapers&rsquo; coverage of local crimes is stronger than that of non-local crimes.<br />\nNothing is said about frequency of coverage so, we can not assume anything about it. However, all else being equal, we can assume that people tend to think the local crimes outnumber non-local crimes.</p>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The value of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5d554f17ca74f8d1bd33.png\" /> is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2"},{"key":"B","html":"7"},{"key":"C","html":"8"},{"key":"D","html":"33"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/aa/aaa4940bdd8b88c1c966.png\" /></div>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The difference between the compound interest and the simple interest earned on a sum of money at the end of 4 years is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/46/465b74f21b5aecea6328.png\" /> To find out the sum, which of the following information given in the statements <em>P</em> and <em>Q</em> is/are necessary?<br />\nP : Amount of simple interest accrued after 4 years.<br />\nQ : Rate of interest per annum.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only P is necessary"},{"key":"B","html":"Only Q is necessary"},{"key":"C","html":"Either P or Q is necessary"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither P nor Q is necessary"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nTo find the sum, difference between C.I. and S.I., the time and the rate of interest are needed.<br />\n&there4; Only Q is necessary.</p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A boat takes 19 hours for travelling downstream from point A to point B and coming back to a point C midway between A and B. If the velocity of the stream is 4 kmph and the speed of the boat in still water is 14 kmph, what is the distance between A and B?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"160 km"},{"key":"B","html":"180 km"},{"key":"C","html":"200 km"},{"key":"D","html":"220 km"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/35/35bf24fa038429ae0e8b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Given below are 4 pairs of words. In three of the pairs, the words are having a meaning similar to each other whereas in one of the pairs, it is the opposite. Identify the option where the words do not have similar meaning.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"CONTRAPTION - SIMPLE"},{"key":"B","html":"JOCUND - MERRY"},{"key":"C","html":"INSCRUTABLE - ENIGMATIC"},{"key":"D","html":"LOUCHE - INDECENT"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nContraption means a complex arrangement.</p>"},{"n":11,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A random variable <em>X</em> takes non-negative integer values and has probability mass function</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/dc/dc7fa4453c4afc59f6f1.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>What is the expectation <em>E</em>[<em>X</em>]?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9b/9bb8d8c8e08ffa556b1f.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"1"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e9/e99ba8cfa3565f518454.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b6/b6c2d5257cec04a98313.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fa/faafe497d60fb85b65e1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":12,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following system of linear equation:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/96/966c4ad3df8fd4051b11.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>The values of p for which the system of equations have infinitely many solutions is/are</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"14"},{"key":"B","html":"1"},{"key":"C","html":"12"},{"key":"D","html":"2"}],"answer":["B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/87/87684a4d303ad63e3316.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5b/5ba85f85372fc0ae1854.png\" /></div>"},{"n":13,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Let <em>T</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>T</em><sub>2</sub> be the worst case running times to search for an element in a binary search tree with <em>n</em>-elements and a balanced binary search tree with n2 2n elements. Then <em>T</em><sub>1</sub> + <em>T</em><sub>2</sub> will be equal to</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Ο(2<em><sup>n</sup></em>)"},{"key":"B","html":"Ο(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup>)"},{"key":"C","html":"Ο(<em>n</em> ⋅ 2<em><sup>n</sup></em>)"},{"key":"D","html":"Ο(<em>n</em>)"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6b/6b2ac0a773d975905f7d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":14,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the Cumulative Distribution Function (CDF) of a random variable <em>X</em> :</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3b/3bad85b70a906af7e69d.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Find the value of <em>P</em>(<em>X</em><sup>2</sup> ≤ 1)?</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.5,0.5],"answerText":"0.5","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.5</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f9/f96eb9ce08bfd43582e5.png\" /></div>"},{"n":15,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider function <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) = (<em>x</em><sup>2</sup> – 9)2 where <em>x</em> is read number then the function has</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"function <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) has only one minima."},{"key":"B","html":"function <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) has only two minima."},{"key":"C","html":"function <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) has only one maxima."},{"key":"D","html":"function <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) has only two maxima."}],"answer":["B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/80/807d04b372d993bc5650.png\" /></div>"},{"n":16,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>If you execute my_tuple = (10, 20, 30), which line of code will result in a Python TypeError?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Attempting to change an element: my_tuple[1] = 25."},{"key":"B","html":"Converting to a list: my_list = list(my_tuple)."},{"key":"C","html":"Accessing an element: print(my_tuple[0])."},{"key":"D","html":"Concatenating two tuples: new_tuple = my_tuple + (40)."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nTuples are immutable, so attempting to change an element by assignment (modification in-place)<br />\nwill raise a TypeError.</p>"},{"n":17,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Feature extraction can help improve model performance when the original features are noisy or highly correlated.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Feature selection reduces overfitting, improves model interpretability, and decreases computational cost.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n&bull; Feature extraction (e.g., PCA) transforms correlated or noisy features into new uncorrelated features, often improving model performance. So, <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is correct.<br />\n&bull; Feature selection chooses a subset of original features, which reduces overfitting, improves<br />\ninterpretability, and lowers computational cost. So, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is correct.</p>"},{"n":18,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following is the most appropriate logical formula to represent the statement:<br />\n“There is a atleast one funny comedian”<br />\nThe following notations are used.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/80/8039b621697c62b7338e.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a2/a2df574ab8ec7593668a.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/53/5375de5cd80e16e49065.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9e/9eb3e80e0c53649de4aa.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/57/572edb1c7c7d8f50cf30.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e0/e06aec1b6998dbd63ebb.png\" /></div>"},{"n":19,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider two relations <em>R</em><sub>1</sub>(<em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, <em>C</em>) and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, <em>C</em>) with the same schema. If these relations are combined using set union, which of the following statements is true regarding functional dependencies?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"All functional dependencies of <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> are always preserved."},{"key":"B","html":"Some functional dependencies may be lost across the combined relation."},{"key":"C","html":"Set union automatically enforces all original dependencies."},{"key":"D","html":"Set union is equivalent to join for preserving dependencies."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n&bull; Set Union combines tuples from relations with the same schema.<br />\n&bull; However, it does not enforce functional dependencies that depend on relationships across<br />\ntuples from different relations.<br />\n&bull; Therefore, some FDs may be lost after union, even if the schema matches.</p>"},{"n":20,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>In a hash function <em>h</em>(<em>k</em>) = key mod <em>m</em>, where <em>m</em> is the size of the table. Then choose the best possible ‘<em>m</em>’ in 3, 7, 9, 11 and following keys are hashed into the 24, 49, 20, 16, 23, 36, 34, 60 hash table, to handle the collision linear probing is used, after inserting all the key if new key is inserted then what is the probability that it hashed into empty slot ________. [Upto 2 decimal places]</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.27,0.27],"answerText":"0.27","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.27</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/54/54987b1d6b385802372c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":21,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following conditions is NOT a requirement for A* search to be complete (i.e., guaranteed to find a solution if one exists)?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The path to the goal must have a finite cost."},{"key":"B","html":"The search space must have a finite branching factor (<em>b</em> < ∞)."},{"key":"C","html":"The cost of every step must be greater than zero (ε > 0)."},{"key":"D","html":"The heuristic function <em>h</em>(<em>n</em>) must be admissible."}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nAdmissibility is required for optimality, not completeness. A* is complete even with a nonadmissible<br />\nheuristic, provided the other conditions are met.</p>"},{"n":22,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>For function <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) = 2<em>x</em><sup>3</sup> – 15<em>x</em><sup>2</sup> + 36<em>x</em> + 1, the maximum occurs in interval [1, 5], when <em>x</em> is equal to</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2"},{"key":"B","html":"3"},{"key":"C","html":"1"},{"key":"D","html":"5"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5b/5b5a286446fb096d85de.png\" /></div>"},{"n":23,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>In a selection process involving three student Niraj, Rahul and Sneha. It is known that exactly one of them will be chosen. Niraj and Rahul are likely to be selected, where as Sneha has only half the probability of Niraj of being selected. What is the probability that the student selected is either Niraj or Sneha?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.6,0.6],"answerText":"0.6","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.6 [0.6 - 0.6]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4a/4a58c908d3248e27a777.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/19/19cf730f9bd49368124f.png\" /></div>"},{"n":24,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : A model with High Bias is often characterized by being underfit to the training data, meaning it is too simple to capture the underlying relationship.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) uses the gradient calculated from all the training examples in the dataset to perform a single weight update.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\n&bull; A high-bias model is too simple to capture the true relationship in the data. It fails to fit the training data well, resulting in underfitting. So, <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is correct.<br />\n&bull; Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) updates weights using only one or a few examples at a<br />\ntime. Using all training examples is Batch Gradient Descent, not SGD. So, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":25,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Let <em>f</em>(<em>n</em>) and <em>g</em>(<em>n</em>) be functions such that <em>f</em>(<em>n</em>) = Ο(<em>g</em>(<em>n</em>)).<br />\nAssume all logarithms are bass 3.<br />\nConsider the following statements:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/55/554fb7d1db5498102c35.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the above statements is/are correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/76/76dc8587b4c14b8b19bd.png\" /></div>"},{"n":26,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following is true about linked list implementation of queue?<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : In enqueue operation. If new node is inserted at beginning of linked list, then in dequeue operation must be removed from end.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : In enqueue operation. If new node is inserted at the end, then in dequeue operation node must be removed from the beginning.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nTo keep the first in first out order a queue can be implemented using linked list in 2 ways.<br />\n1. If a new node is inserted at the beginning of linked list, then in dequeue operation node must<br />\nbe removed from end.<br />\n2. If new node is inserted at the end of linked list then, in dequeue operation, node must be<br />\nremoved from beginning.</p>"},{"n":27,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following relation:<br />\nR(P, Q, R, S, T, U) with FD set of relation R(P → Q, R → S, T → U)<br />\nWhat is the minimum number of relation required to decompose relation R into BCNF which satisfy lossless join and dependency preserving decomposition.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[4,4],"answerText":"4","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>4</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ef/efd53a3265a3be325edd.png\" /></div>"},{"n":28,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The term ‘slow mixing’ in Gibbs Sampling refers to a high degree of autocorrelation between successive samples. This typically occurs when:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The total number of collected samples is very small."},{"key":"B","html":"The burn-in period is set too long."},{"key":"C","html":"The conditional distributions are non-conjugate priors."},{"key":"D","html":"The dimensions (variables) of the joint distribution are highly correlated with each other."}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nSlow mixing in Gibbs Sampling occurs when the variables in the joint distribution are highly<br />\ncorrelated with each other. In such cases, updating one variable at a time results in very small<br />\nmovements in the state space, causing successive samples to be very similar. This creates high<br />\nautocorrelation and makes the Markov chain explore the distribution very slowly.<br />\nTherefore, strong inter-variable correlation is the main reason for slow mixing.</p>"},{"n":29,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following operations, commonly associated with the OLAP analytical environment, involves reducing the dimensionality of the data by aggregating or summarizing values along a dimension hierarchy</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Roll-Up"},{"key":"B","html":"Slice and Dice"},{"key":"C","html":"Pivot"},{"key":"D","html":"Drill-Down"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nRoll-Up (or consolidation) climbs up the dimension hierarchy, aggregating data across lower levels to present a higher-level summary, such as combining sales from individual stores into regional sales.</p>"},{"n":30,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a file of 32768 records and its key field is of size 8 bytes. This key is not used for file ordering. Some other key is used for ordering.<br />\nEach record size is 32 bytes.<br />\nBlock size of 1024 bytes use in file. System and block pointer size is 12 bytes. Two level index scheme used in file system. The number of blocks used indexing (not data storage) to store this file is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[656,656],"answerText":"656","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>656</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b4/b41043b3b02f2f54fdbb.png\" /></div>"},{"n":31,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Given a dataset for an <em>M</em> class classification task, with <em>K</em> categorical attributes where each attribute can take <em>V</em> distinct values (<em>K</em> ≥ 1, <em>V</em> ≥ 2, <em>M</em> ≥ 2), what is the number of independent parameters that must be estimated for learning a Naive Bayes classifier?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>K</em>(<em>M</em> – 1) + <em>M</em>(<em>V</em> – 1)"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>KMV</em> – 1"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>KM</em>(<em>V</em> – 1) + (<em>M</em> – 1)"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>KMV</em>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nThis correctly sums the M &ndash; 1 independent prior class probabilities and <em>K</em> &times; <em>M</em> &times; (<em>V</em> &ndash; 1) independent<br />\nconditional probabilities required for the features.</p>"},{"n":32,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following two statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : A student in the college has not submitted the assignment.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Every student in the college attended the orientation session.<br />\nWhich of the following follows from <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> as per sound inference rule of logic?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Every student who attended the orientation session has submitted the assignment."},{"key":"B","html":"Someone who attended the orientation session has not submitted the assignment."},{"key":"C","html":"Everyone who attended the orientation session has not submitted the assignment."},{"key":"D","html":"Someone who attended the orientation session has submitted the assignment."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d4/d4c155ed87e0180cc216.png\" /></div>"},{"n":33,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Let <em>A</em> be a 3 × 3 real matrix satisfying</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e9/e9e0d99dd5012580cce4.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>If det(<em>A</em>) = 8, then what is the value of trace(<em>A</em>) ________?</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[8,8],"answerText":"8","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>8</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c0/c040bc855b34693a5087.png\" /></div>"},{"n":34,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The decision boundary learned by a Naive Bayes classifier (for two classes) is generally linear in feature space when the conditional feature distributions are:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Any distribution, as the model is inherently linear."},{"key":"B","html":"Bernoulli or Multinomial with equal variance/smoothing parameters for all classes."},{"key":"C","html":"Gaussian with class-dependent means."},{"key":"D","html":"Gaussian with class-dependent variances."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nFor discrete features, the decision boundary is piecewise linear. When the feature distributions<br />\nare the same across classes, the log-ratio simplifies to a linear form.</p>"},{"n":35,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A call center receives 100 calls. The duration of each call is an independent random variable <em>X<sub>i </sub></em>with mean is 4 minutes and standard deviation is 3 minutes.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/07/07a48f71d7e636d6951c.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>denote the total duration (in minutes) of these 100 calls.<br />\nUsing the central limit theorem, the approximate value of <em>P</em>(<em>Y</em> ≤ 450) is ________?\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/77/77570696246bd1beb3dd.png\" /></div>\n</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6c/6cfcf1f4af992f621d0b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":36,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following schema:<br />\nDepartment (dept_ID, dept_name, dept_Location)<br />\nEmployee (emp_ID, emp_Name, emp_DeptID, emp_salary)<br />\nemp_DeptID is a foreign key referring to dept_ID of the department relation. Assume that each<br />\ndepartment has atleast one employee.<br />\nWhat does the following query return?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4e/4eb02275e7fa5803bfa4.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Name of all department where all employees earn more than or equal to 50000."},{"key":"B","html":"Name of all department where atleast one employee earns more than 50,000."},{"key":"C","html":"Name of all department where none of the employees earn more than 50,000."},{"key":"D","html":"Name of all departments where all employees earn exactly 50,000."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\n&bull; Inner sub query identifies all the employees in a particular department (emp_DeptID) whose<br />\nsalary is less than 50000. It retrieve emp_ID for those employee where the emp_DepID match<br />\nthe dept_ID from the outer query.<br />\n&bull; Outer query select the dept_name from the department relation. The condition is based on the<br />\nNOT EXISTS clause, which means the outer query will only return department for which the<br />\ninner query finds no employees with a salary less than 50,000. So, all employee earn 50,000.<br />\nSo, option (a) is correct.</p>"},{"n":37,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A sample of 7 people in an office showed daily consumption of 8, 12, 10, 9, 11, 14, 6 unit of a product. Calculate the 90% confidence interval for the average daily consumption.<br />\nGiven <em>t</em> = 1.943 for 6 degree of freedom.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"(8.057, 6.058)"},{"key":"B","html":"(8.057, 11.943)"},{"key":"C","html":"(7.63, 12.12)"},{"key":"D","html":"(7.02, 11.943)"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/71/7164a5ef9ca1c4f8c7b9.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e1/e146ab6156011dae26ff.png\" /></div>"},{"n":38,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A machine learning model is trained on a dataset. It achieves an almost zero training error, but when evaluated on a test dataset, the error is extremely high. This behavior suggests that the model fits the training data very well but fails to generalize to unseen data.<br />\nWhich of the following options best describes the state of this model?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"High Bias and Low Variance"},{"key":"B","html":"High Bias and High Variance"},{"key":"C","html":"Low Bias and High Variance"},{"key":"D","html":"Low Bias and Low Variance"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nSince the model achieves almost zero training error, it fits the training data very well, indicating<br />\nlow bias. However, the extremely high test error shows that it fails to generalize to unseen data,<br />\nwhich is a clear sign of high variance. This behavior is typical of an overfitted model.</p>"},{"n":39,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The time and space complexity of the Variable Elimination algorithm is generally expressed as Ο(<em>n</em> ⋅ <em>k<sup>w</sup></em>), where n is the number of variables, <em>k</em> is the maximum cardinality of any variable, and <em>w </em>represents the maximum size of any intermediate factor (minus one) generated during the process.<br />\nThis w is also known as the:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Maximum Clique Size in the original Moral Graph"},{"key":"B","html":"Width of the Elimination Order (Treewidth)"},{"key":"C","html":"Number of Hidden Variables"},{"key":"D","html":"Depth of the Bayes Net"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nThe computational complexity of exact inference algorithms like Variable Elimination is exponential<br />\nin the treewidth of the network, which corresponds to the size of the largest clique formed<br />\nduring the elimination process.</p>"},{"n":40,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider following element 99, 29, 50, 27, 22, 20, 12, 15, 17, 21, 16, 19, 80 are inserted into empty heap tree in same sequence as given.<br />\nWhat is the maximum number of interchanges needed so, that resulted tree is a max heap?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1"},{"key":"B","html":"2"},{"key":"C","html":"3"},{"key":"D","html":"4"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a7/a7d0716b7363b7c3f200.png\" /></div>"},{"n":41,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>An array of 128 elements is sorted using merge sort. If the total number of recursive calls made by merge sort is defined as all calls excluding the initial call, find the exact number of recursive calls.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"254"},{"key":"B","html":"255"},{"key":"C","html":"256"},{"key":"D","html":"127"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2c/2cc1324ef58e26a41a7c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":42,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>An alpha(α) cutoff (or beta (α) cutoff) occurs at a Min node when the found in a child node V, and the current alpha( ) and beta( ) are such that:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>V</em> > β"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>V</em> > α"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>V</em> ≤ α"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>V</em> ≤ β"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nIf the value V returned by a child is less than or equal to &alpha;, then Max ancestor already has a better<br />\nguaranteed move, so this branch is proven irrelevant and pruned.</p>"},{"n":43,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a two-class classification problem in <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/4b/4b084c0cff1bd628769c.png\" /> with μ<sub>1</sub> and μ<sub>2</sub>. A classifier assigns a sample<br />\n<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/09/09d7f6670146fc22e09f.png\" /> to the class whose mean is closest to <em>x</em> in terms of Euclidean distance.<br />\nThe decision boundary is the set of all points <em>x</em> for which the distance to μ<sub>1</sub> equals the distance μ<sub>2</sub>:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/ff/ff6b0c1165576ed5fa46.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>This decision boundary can be described by the equation <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/fc/fc8327fbbda7a508be03.png\" /> is the normal vector to the boundary and <em>k</em> is a constant.<br />\nWhich of the following statements about the decision boundary is/are correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The decision boundary is a hyperplane that is always perpendicular (orthogonal) to the vector connecting the two means, (μ<sub>1</sub> – μ<sub>2</sub>)."},{"key":"B","html":"The normal vector w to the decision boundary is proportional to the difference between the means, i.e., w ∝ (μ<sub>1</sub> – μ<sub>2</sub>)."},{"key":"C","html":"The decision boundary always passes through the midpoint of the line segment connecting μ<sub>1</sub> and μ<sub>2</sub>, which is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/37/37d0e86e49bdb4ae8a82.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"The decision boundary for this classifier is a quadratic surface, such as a sphere or an ellipsoid."}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1f/1f2c96a1062e33381ba0.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/87/8791e010aeaf3c791f10.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":44,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p><img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/73/73b4fa18b4534d1bc9bb.png\" /></p>\n\n<p>The value of the above limit is _______?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.5,0.5],"answerText":"0.5","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.5</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d1/d16694f63f9f0f743daa.png\" /></div>"},{"n":45,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Let a quicksort algorithm <em>Q</em> sort numbers in ascending order using the first element as the pivot in every recursive call.<br />\nLet <em>c</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>c</em><sub>2</sub> be the number of comparisons made by <em>Q</em> on the following inputs:<br />\nInput A: [2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12]<br />\nInput B: [6, 2, 10, 4, 12, 8]<br />\nWhich ONE of the following is TRUE?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/70/70c97d020a089f54d9ac.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/87/87de644b43f4e756b7f3.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/30/303a211e1235e52f8854.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"No comparison difference can be determined from the inputs"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/62/622a0ccb37eea91973ee.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9f/9ffd33d9ed56c4da9e8b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":46,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Let <em>X</em> be a continuous random variable with cumulative distribution function:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ab/ab1d5b33e8fa219b2ea5.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>If the median at <em>X</em> is 4, what is the value of a?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"–4"},{"key":"B","html":"–2"},{"key":"C","html":"2"},{"key":"D","html":"4"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3e/3e10262f601df2ca55ec.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":47,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Suppose a Min-Heap H stores exactly 64 keys. The height of H is defined as the maximum number of edges on a path from the root to any leaf.<br />\nWhat is the height of H ________? (Answer in integer)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[6,6],"answerText":"6","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>6</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/63/630fbb2016219c3c3964.png\" /></div>"},{"n":48,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following weighted undirected graph. Which of the following sequences of edges represents correct execution of Kruskal’s algorithm to construct a minimum spanning tree?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2f/2fcacb935fed05ab8953.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a9/a9fc508c3ac7efb5702c.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/92/924c5d998b6341ea7239.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/74/7427db9552bb4a35748d.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b5/b572d22f676284a6e728.png\" />"}],"answer":["C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c, d)<br />\n(a) has cycle.<br />\n(b) (<em>d</em>, <em>e</em>) is not a part of MST.</p>"},{"n":49,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Two matrixes <em>A</em> and <em>B</em> are given below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5d4e534c15f6f75337d2.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>If the rank of matrix <em>A</em> is <em>N</em>, then the rank of matrix <em>B</em> is</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1f/1ff97e9b44c730f850b9.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>N</em> – 1"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>N</em>"},{"key":"D","html":"2<em>N</em>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/72/72ec4a436f11b6570014.png\" /></div>"},{"n":50,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Data are pushed to (PUSH operation) and popped from (POP operation) a stack in the following order:<br />\nPUSH 3; TOP; PUSH 8; PUSH 7; TOP; PUSH 6; PUSH 9; TOP; POP; POP; TOP;<br />\nwhere the PUSH, POP and TOP are standard operations of stack.<br />\nWhich of the values returned by TOP in order for the sequence of operations above?<br />\n[<strong>Note:</strong> TOP is an operation that returns the value of the topmost element of the stack without deleting it from the stack.]</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3, 7, 9, 7"},{"key":"B","html":"3, 7, 9, 6"},{"key":"C","html":"3, 8, 9, 6"},{"key":"D","html":"3, 8, 9, 7"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":51,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p><img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/fc/fce2123970870d9e0d79.png\" /></p>\n\n<p>Let dim(U) denote the dimension of subspace U. Which of the following statements are TRUE?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3b/3b074b4c1a7c57daa984.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/aa/aa1bb9b19f40f7d5984f.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4f/4ff488c55e4ff8787bf1.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b0/b03ba0e097445dc0d337.png\" />"}],"answer":["A","B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bc/bc1fd9410b1871bf38ad.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/71/714e187597a5eab2b3c5.png\" /></div>"},{"n":52,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n(a) A be skew-symmetric matrix of odd order, then determinant is zero.<br />\n(b) Maximum number of distinct eigen values of matrix <em>A</em> < size of matrix.<br />\n(c) For all 3 × 3 matrices <em>A</em> and <em>B</em>, <em>AB</em> = <em>BA</em><br />\n(d) If you have an <em>m</em> × <em>m</em> matrix A that is invertible then, the column of A form a basis for R<sup>m</sup>.<br />\nWhich of the above statement is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, d)<br />\n&bull; Skew symmetric matrix is odd order then determinant is always zero. So, option (a) is correct.<br />\n&bull; Maximum number of distinct eigen values = size of matrix <em>A</em>. So, option (b) is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; Matrix multiplication is not commutative thus, <em>AB</em> &ne; <em>BA</em>. So, option (c) is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; <em>A</em><sub><em>m</em> &times; <em>m</em></sub> which is invertible thus |<em>A</em>| &ne; 0 i.e., linear combinations of columns space of A covers<br />\nthe whole Rm. So, option (d) is correct.</p>"},{"n":53,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>A dataset consists of the following values:<br />\n5.5, 7.25, 13.5, 6, 15, 3.5, 14, 10<br />\nWe want to divide the data into 2 clusters using k-means clustering. The initial centroids are<br />\n<em>k</em><sub>1</sub> = 6 and <em>k</em><sub>2</sub> = 12.<br />\nAfter the first iteration, the centroids are updated. How many data points will change clusters in the second iteration?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0,0],"answerText":"0","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8f/8fd056300834e6276b6d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":54,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Let <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> be two relations with the following schema:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/23/23ed048c9b492324a6bf.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/31/319670dd50bcd694f496.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6f/6f2f515f640258783aaf.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2c/2cd5f4787bf37af1f971.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e0/e0beca3fbab8eb5d6c8a.png\" />"}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/59/59cc28d05e9b61999368.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/af/af64a52c58e0b1f81eef.png\" /></div>"},{"n":55,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the neural network with the ReLU activation function, <em>R</em>(<em>x</em>) = max(0, <em>x</em>).<br />\nLet the inputs be u and v, and the output be y. The network equations are:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ab/abc45772b7ba7587c458.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following is correct?</div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6d/6d5a8297873b3968b4cf.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3c/3c712478158c0d5da37d.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/17/1762bc646992c48b3c40.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9d/9d12344c37c7c077cb08.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ab/ab84babbe18c523e5ac6.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8b/8beaa705ca7ffe3a8899.png\" /></div>"},{"n":56,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"In a directed acyclic graph (DAG), performing DFS always produces no back edges."},{"key":"B","html":"BFS on an undirected graph can produce cross edges, but never back edges."},{"key":"C","html":"Bellman-Ford algorithm can correctly find shortest paths in a graph with negative edges but no negative cycles."},{"key":"D","html":"Prim’s algorithm may fail on a graph with negative edge weights."}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c)<br />\n&bull; A back edge in DFS goes from a node to its ancestor. If a graph has a cycle, DFS produces a<br />\nback edge. A DAG has no cycles, so DFS on a DAG cannot have back edges. So, option (a) is<br />\ncorrect.<br />\n&bull; In BFS for undirected graphs, all non-tree edges connect either:<br />\n(i) current level &rarr; previous level &rarr; considered a back edge<br />\n(ii) current level &rarr; same level &rarr; considered a cross edge<br />\nSo BFS may have back edges in undirected graphs, and calling them &ldquo;cross edges only&rdquo; is not<br />\ncorrect. So, option (b) is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; Bellman-Ford works for graphs with negative edges as long as there is no negative cycle. It<br />\niteratively relaxes edges to compute correct shortest distances. So, option (c) is correct.<br />\n&bull; Prim&rsquo;s algorithm works fine even with negative edge weights, because it only picks the<br />\nminimum weight edge at each step to grow the MST. Negative edges do not affect correctness.<br />\nSo, option (d) is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":57,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the cycle graph <em>C</em><sub>5</sub> such that each edge is uniquely assigned a weight from the set {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}. Let wmin be the weight of MST of this graph. Let S denote the set of all spanning trees for <em>C</em><sub>5</sub>. An element x is picked at random from S, having weight <em>w</em>. Then the probability that <em>w</em> > <em>w</em><sub>min </sub>is equal to ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.8,0.8],"answerText":"0.8","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.8</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/df/df27613b19ad6fa5405e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":58,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following system of linear equations:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/34/34e0d0c8f83c08163705.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following is/are correct for this system of equation?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"It has unique solution"},{"key":"B","html":"It has infinite solution"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/58/58befd0e36456a1d3904.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d5/d5593a99818f32320bc0.png\" />"}],"answer":["B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, c, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/22/22cd9aa60686b4a9f832.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":59,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a matrix <em>A</em>:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0f/0f616daa70da3b6c11fd.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>If <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/84/84cd576cd7d1c62a3c09.png\" /> then which of the following represent the matrix <em>B</em>?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/53/53c49da4ecd06e66ab0b.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/29/29df58bb5238051accba.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/51/51cf09d95920e3e69b49.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/87/87b3760fccd4117d43e3.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fb/fbf224ece978057d840e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":60,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A queue Q supports the usual operations enqueue(x) to add an element and dequeue( ) to remove<br />\nand return an element.<br />\nConsider the following Python code segment:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/93/93686ad214f1d1b33535.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>What will be the value of the variable count when the above code finishes execution?</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[12,12],"answerText":"12","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>12<br />\nThe queue starts with the value 1, and in every iteration the dequeued value x generates two new<br />\nvalues: 2* x and 4* x.<br />\nAs the queue processes numbers in increasing order, the sequence of dequeued values becomes:<br />\n1, 2, 4, 4, 8, 8, 16, 8, 16, 16, 32.<br />\nThe 11th dequeue gives x = 32, so the loop stops at that moment.<br />\nSince count starts at 1 and increases once per iteration, the final value becomes 12.</p>"},{"n":61,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>If all edge costs in a graph are identical and equal to 1, how does UCS behave?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"It becomes equivalent to Depth-First Search (DFS)."},{"key":"B","html":"It becomes equivalent to Breadth-First Search (BFS)."},{"key":"C","html":"It becomes equivalent to the A* search algorithm."},{"key":"D","html":"It becomes an inefficient, randomized search process."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nWhen every edge in the graph has the same cost (cost = 1), UCS expands nodes in order of<br />\nincreasing path cost, which basically becomes the same as exploring nodes level by level. That&rsquo;s<br />\nexactly how BFS works. So with uniform edge costs, UCS behaves just like BFS, because both end<br />\nup expanding the shallowest unvisited nodes first</p>"},{"n":62,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"If a random variable <em>X</em> satisfies <em>E</em>[<em>X</em>] = 0 then <em>E</em>[<em>X</em><sup>3</sup>] = 0."},{"key":"B","html":"For any two independent random variables <em>X</em> and <em>Y</em>, var(<em>X</em> + <em>Y</em>) = var(<em>X</em>) + var(<em>Y</em>)."},{"key":"C","html":"For events <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/02/02cc35fe7cde5e409052.png\" /> are positively correlated."},{"key":"D","html":"For a random variable <em>X</em>, <em>E</em>(<em>X</em><sup>2</sup>) ≥ [<em>E</em>(<em>X</em>)]<sup>2</sup>."}],"answer":["B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, c, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3b/3b20c1a2ef522446e721.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d8/d8c1aface175e6bd5e43.png\" /></div>"},{"n":63,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Let G be a connected undirected graph with positive edge weights, and let <em>T</em> be one of its minimum spanning trees.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b8/b86be91abfb0bc1c6c83.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following statements is always true?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/70/70df79b5b67bf349276d.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/19/1935b2d0b8329b889fc9.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/14/14caf7e0e17b9a16ec00.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/25/25354f9c26c9bd61a475.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/52/527c82ba6d278a37eda1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":64,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The value of integral given below is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/53/53a3ca88de11a3984792.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"ln(54)"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cd/cdc03b1c190a99a5303a.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"ln(216)"},{"key":"D","html":"0"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/57/577da5792c45fbe0c8f8.png\" /></div>"},{"n":65,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>In a logistic regression model with L2 regularization, the weight vector is <em>w</em> = [2, –1, 5] and the regularization parameter is λ = 0.6. Calculate the L2 penalty.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"9"},{"key":"B","html":"9.8"},{"key":"C","html":"8.4"},{"key":"D","html":"7.2"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/13/13b01a321d7677d1116f.png\" /></div>"}],"math":false,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"General Aptitude","start":0,"count":10},{"name":"Technical","start":10,"count":55}],"stats":{"questions":65,"maxMarks":100,"types":{"MCQ":43,"NAT":12,"MSQ":10},"withoutKey":0}};
